# Local environment values for runs outside CI.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this example is not, so it must
# never carry a real value.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Who reads it: the Makefile's build-diagnostic and verify-iso-datastore targets
# source it, and infrastructure/scripts/Get-VCenterDiscovery.ps1 reads it
# itself. make is not installed on the Windows workstation, so for the runner
# scripts and govc, load it into the shell first:
#     Git Bash:    set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#     PowerShell:  see "Loading .env in PowerShell" in AGENT_HANDOFF.md
# In CI these same values arrive as GitLab CI/CD variables, so .env has no
# effect there.
#
# The names below are the ones the scripts actually read. They are checked by
# scripts/runner/build-diagnostic.sh before a build starts, and any name that
# does not match exactly is treated as unset.

# QUOTE ANY VALUE CONTAINING A BACKSLASH. This file is read with `source`, and
# bash treats a backslash in an unquoted assignment as an escape character, so
#     VCENTER_USERNAME=DOMAIN\user
# silently becomes DOMAINuser and vCenter answers "incorrect user name or
# password" — which sends you looking at the password. Single-quote it, or use
# the UPN form user@domain, which has no backslash to lose. The same applies to
# any password containing \ $ or a backtick.
#
# CI is unaffected: GitLab injects these directly, with no shell parsing. This
# trap only exists for values read from this file.

# --- vCenter ---
# Hostname only, no scheme. The scripts add https:// where they need it.
VCENTER_HOST=
# 'DOMAIN\user' (quoted) or user@domain. See the warning above.
VCENTER_USERNAME=
VCENTER_PASSWORD=

# --- Build identity ---
# The administrative service account the guest is joined and provisioned with.
# ADMIN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT is the bare account name, without a domain prefix.
ADMIN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=
ADMIN_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PASSWORD=

# --- Active Directory ---
# AD_DOMAIN is the DNS domain (example: example.test).
# AD_NETBIOS_DOMAIN is the short NetBIOS name (example: EXAMPLE), used to build
# the DOMAIN\user form that WinRM authenticates with.
AD_DOMAIN=
AD_NETBIOS_DOMAIN=

# --- Runner network ---
# The IPv4 address the guest firewall opens WinRM HTTPS to. The Packer template
# validates this as an IPv4 address, so a hostname will not work.
PACKER_RUNNER_SOURCE_IP=

# --- Guest administrators ---
# The domain group mapped into the guest's local Administrators during
# specialize. ADMIN_GROUP_SID is what the bootstrap acts on and must be a domain
# SID (S-1-5-21-x-y-z-rid); a SID rather than a name because name resolution is
# unreliable that early after the domain join. ADMIN_GROUP_NAME is the
# DOMAIN\GROUP form and is only written to the guest's build log.
#
# The lab values, and how the SID was derived and verified, are recorded in
# configs/windows-11-vsphere.pkrvars.hcl.example. Neither is a secret. A SID
# rather than a name because name resolution is not reliably available during
# the specialize pass; that is observed, not precautionary.
#
# The SID is environment-specific: it names one group in one domain. Another
# environment must resolve and approve its own, not reuse the lab's.
#
# This mapping survives into every clone of the image, so pre-seal cleanup has
# to remove it (ADR 0002).
ADMIN_GROUP_SID=
ADMIN_GROUP_NAME=
